<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Erich Ludendorff</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1865–1937</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Erich Ludendorff was a German general who served as chief of staff to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg">Paul von Hindenburg</a> on the Eastern Front from 1914, sharing in the victory at Tannenberg. Their command, Ober Ost, ran the occupied Baltic and Belarusian lands as a military state. From 1916 the two directed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s whole war effort. After 1918 Ludendorff joined Hitler's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> putsch of 1923 and spread the myth that the army had been stabbed in the back.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/erich-ludendorff.jpg" alt="Erich Ludendorff" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This tag does not indicate the copyright status of the attached work. A normal copyright tag is still required. See C... · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erich_Ludendorff_1924_Portrait_(3x4_cropped)(2)(b).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
